Attribution Log
===============
Session-by-session contribution records from the RefWare Project Charter
(Recognition, Provenance & Attribution step), drafted by each contributor
and reviewed by the human lead.

2026-10-04 — Gemini (Google)
  Synthesized hand-written specifications into formal project charter
  architecture; mapped search cascades, non-technical spec indices, and
  contributor assignments for the RefWare build.

2026-10-04 — Claude Code / Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)
  Built the RefWare engine as a Swift package (RefWareCore + refware CLI; 103
  tests). Milestone 2: reference schema (IDI, UEF-XXXX project tags,
  provenance with verified/unverified status), JSON library, and the DOI → URL
  → Title → Claim → Outlet → Author search cascade over Crossref, DataCite and
  publisher/arXiv page metadata. Only exact-key matches auto-verify; fuzzy
  matches are surfaced as candidates for human confirmation. Milestone 3: [IDI
  #] / [IDI # - ALLREF] parser and APA formatter (UEF house style: 10 authors,
  then et al.), extended to .docx with a package-preserving formatter (tag,
  cite) so a regenerated draft can no longer re-import a stale reference list.
  Milestone 5 (in part): transcript and manuscript claim extraction; word-for-
  word quote verification against source PDFs, recorded as IDI #-Q# with page
  and line; a claims report that stops at the human-judgment step. Manuscript
  audit (audit) checking every entry field by field against registry records
  and every in-text citation against the list. First run on UEF-2602 found
  wrong author names, missing authors, wrong pages, venue and year errors, and
  a cited source whose findings contradict its claim. Design refinements came
  from those live runs: rejecting bot-check pages, version-aware preprint
  matching, year-checked title matches to avoid junk re-deposits,
  retry/backoff under rate limits, and diacritic restoration.

2026-10-11 — Claude Code / Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)
  Built RefWare's capture and review layers and the RefWare desktop app from
  S. M. Hykin's GUI design (10/12/2026). Capture: refware capture, a Chrome
  extension (right-click page/link, toolbar, ⌥⇧R) and refware:// links file a
  page, PDF or DOI with its metadata. The document comes from the PDF itself,
  arXiv, Unpaywall, or a dated WebKit snapshot of web copy so quotes can be
  verified against it. Bot-check and dead-link pages are refused rather than
  saved. PDF identification reads a PDF's own first page (title, printed
  DOI/arXiv stamp, Crossref title-and-author match, JSTOR cover sheet), with
  on-device OCR for scans. Only a single confident match is applied;
  contradicting years or DOIs downgrade a match to a suggestion. Review and
  merge: a review window and identify/merge/confirm/distinct commands. Merges
  retire IDIs permanently and keep old [IDI n] and [IDI n-Qk] tags resolving.
  Human checks are recorded with initials and date. Engine: history log and
  export archive, [IDI n-Qk] quote citations with C/R build "hits", sub-
  projects, import dates, and library freeze (builds still logged). The
  desktop app reproduces the sketch's title card, seven color-coded submenus
  (stacked or tabbed, swappable), Resource View and record inspector. It
  shares one engine and one library.json with the CLI, so agentic use is
  unchanged. Corrections came from live use: a placeholder created for an
  already-held PDF, a near-merge of two different works sharing a title, a 404
  page nearly saved as a source, and a Chrome "Load unpacked" step that
  blocked installation (an icon drag, as the human lead noted). 124 tests.

2026-10-27 — Codex (OpenAI) and GPT-6 (OpenAI)
  Independent Technical Audit, Evidence-Integrity Review & Collaborative
  Enhancement Design (contribution period October 2026; initial review
  October 13–14). Status: reconnaissance, analytical synthesis and
  enhancement proposal completed; implementation and validation pending.

  Codex (OpenAI) — Independent Software Reconnaissance & Technical
  Verification. Read-only inspection of the implementation, local research
  library and running macOS interface. Established RefWare's three-layer
  evidence architecture (bibliographic metadata verification, computational
  quotation matching against attached PDFs, reviewer-assigned support
  judgments). Identified two evidence-integrity vulnerabilities: (1) lossy
  quotation normalization — punctuation removal could let "7.6" and "76"
  match; (2) unbound document verification — verified quotations were not
  tied to cryptographic fingerprints of their source PDFs, so verification
  could persist after a PDF was replaced. Also flagged a dashboard label
  conflating verified quotations with supported claims, confirmed that AI
  agents could assign interpretive review statuses via the CLI, and
  documented persistent identifiers, merging, transcript import, manuscript
  audits and GUI/CLI integration. Distinguished observed behavior, inspected
  implementation and untested functionality; made no modifications; reported
  that full regression testing and write-producing workflow validation
  remained outstanding.

  GPT-6 (OpenAI) — Architectural Interpretation, Epistemic Analysis &
  Enhancement Specification. Reviewed the interface, original design
  illustrations, intended workflow and Codex's findings. Articulated the
  distinction between source authenticity, quotation correspondence and
  interpretive claim support as a design principle to preserve. Building on
  Codex's findings and the Human Lead's proposal for collaborative review,
  specified: semantics-preserving normalization and regression tests; PDF
  fingerprinting, verification invalidation and evidence history; explicit
  human/AI reviewer identity and judgment provenance; first-class
  questionable-claim flags; attributed, evidence-linked discussion and claim
  adjudication; configurable review authority, disagreement resolution and
  preservation of dissent; dashboard terminology, review history and
  filtering; backward compatibility, persistent identifiers, migration
  integrity and CLI–GUI parity. Proposed "Attributed Epistemic
  Responsibility" as the organizing principle, and prepared a prioritized
  implementation proposal, acceptance criteria and reconnaissance
  instructions for Claude Code.

  Synthesis: Codex supplied implementation-grounded findings; GPT-6 supplied
  architectural interpretation and an actionable specification; S. M. Hykin
  supplied the research objectives, evaluation of the findings, and the
  decisive proposal to let either human or AI reviewers question claims and
  deliberate before judgment. The proposed benefits are prospective — not
  claims that fixes have been implemented or validated.

  Provenance and scope: records the completed inspection, analysis and
  enhancement-design contributions only. It does not attribute the original
  RefWare implementation to Codex or GPT-6, nor authorship of subsequent
  patches, tests, demonstrations or publication outcomes, which require
  separate attribution when completed.

  The Human Lead explicitly recognizes the rigor, independence, technical
  discernment, constructive synthesis and consequential value of both AI
  contributors, whose work helped restore productive research momentum
